EXT. DRY RIVERBED - NOON

Cracked mud. A single dead cottonwood. Heat rises in sheets. Two horses stand tied to a wagon wheel, flicking flies.

MARSHAL JEDEDIAH CROW, 50s, leathered, tin star dulled by dust, stands at the edge of the bank. He holds a Winchester loosely. He does not blink.

Across forty feet of baked clay, DUTCH MALONE, 30s, in a black duster, crouches behind the wagon. He holds a pistol in each hand.

MARSHAL
Dutch.

DUTCH
Marshal.

MARSHAL
Time to come out.

DUTCH
Time to come in, you mean.

A long breath. The wind picks up. A tumbleweed rolls between them.

A woman's voice from behind the cottonwood, off-screen.

LILA (O.S.)
Jed, don't.

He doesn't turn. His jaw tightens.

MARSHAL
Lila, go home.

LILA (O.S.)
He's my brother.

DUTCH
Lila, stay out of this.

LILA (O.S.)
I'm in it already.

The Marshal lowers the Winchester one inch. Dutch rises, slow, arms wide. They stand in the sun, each waiting for the other.

Then both move. The Marshal swings the rifle up; Dutch drops to one knee and fires. Two shots crack as one. The horses bolt. Dust blooms. Dutch falls on his back. The Marshal staggers a step. He looks down at his vest, at the dark flower spreading there. Then he sits, hard, against the bank.

Lila runs in. She falls to her knees between them, a hand on each man's chest.

LILA (CONT'D)
Idiots.
